#! Position encoding, asserted under utf-8. Three of these files exist and they
#! are NOT copies: the same semantic targets sit at different `character`
#! numbers in each, and a shim that treated all encodings alike would produce
#! three identical transcripts.
#!
#! Under utf-8 `character` is a BYTE column and the shim's conversion is the
#! identity, so a divergence here is a divergence in the compiler's spans
#! rather than in the encoding module. The emoji occupies FOUR columns, so
#! both 19 and the two after it are interior positions with no
#! addressable meaning — the widest version of the clamping case.
#!
#! Both directions are asserted, which is the point of §5:
#!
#!   client position -> offset   the four hover probes on line 24
#!                               (`    let astral = "🐺"`), plus the one on
#!                               line 45 where the target sits AFTER astral,
#!                               BMP, combining and ZWJ text on its own line;
#!   byte span -> client range   the E0003 introduced by the overlay edit,
#!                               whose span the compiler reports in bytes and
#!                               the server must publish at column 44-ish
#!                               in this encoding and elsewhere in the others.
#!
#! The fixture is clean on disk so hover has typechecked code to answer from;
#! the diagnostic is introduced as an UNSAVED edit, which is also how a user
#! meets it.
name      encoding/astral-utf8
profile   utf8-first
workspace fixtures

initialize
open      astral.lu
wait      diagnostics astral.lu
req       hover astral.lu 24:8
req       hover astral.lu 24:18
req       hover astral.lu 24:19
req       hover astral.lu 24:22
req       hover astral.lu 45:44
req       documentSymbol astral.lu
splice    astral.lu 2444 2447 "1 < 2 < 3"
wait      diagnostics astral.lu
req       documentSymbol astral.lu
shutdown
